---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="History of Tamil Literature" description="A complete narrative history of Tamil literature from the Three Sangams to the Medieval period — based on Mu. Varadarajan's Tamil Ilakkiya Varalaru.">

<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="hero-eyebrow">Based on Mu. Varadarajan's Tamil Ilakkiya Varalaru</div>
    <h1>
      <span class="hero-title-ta">தமிழ் இலக்கிய வரலாறு</span>
      <span class="hero-title-en">A History of Tamil Literature</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="hero-intro">Tamil literature does not begin with a single text. It begins with an institutional claim — the legend of three academies, each lasting thousands of years, each city swallowed by the sea. What survives from the third academy is the largest body of ancient secular poetry in any Indian language. This is its history, told without softening.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PERIOD 01 -->
<section class="hist-period" id="three-sangams">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="period-header">
      <span class="period-num">01</span>
      <div>
        <div class="period-tag">Legendary / Pre-Historical</div>
        <h2 class="period-title">The Three Sangams <span class="period-ta">முச்சங்கம்</span></h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="period-body">
      <div class="period-main prose-wide">
        <p class="lead">The Tamil literary tradition's founding legend is not the story of a single poet or a single god. It is the story of an institution — a literary academy (சங்கம்) — sustained across vast stretches of time, under royal patronage, producing literature of which almost nothing survives.</p>

        <h3>The First Sangam — முதல் சங்கம்</h3>
        <p>According to the tradition recorded by later commentators — primarily Nakkirar's commentary on Iraiyanar Akapporul — the First Sangam met at <strong>Tenmaturai</strong> (Southern Madurai), a city since swallowed by the sea. It lasted <strong>4,400 years</strong>. It had <strong>549 members</strong>. It was presided over by <strong>Agastya (அகத்தியர்)</strong> — the mythical sage credited with bringing Tamil civilisation to the south, and author of the lost grammar <em>Agattiyam</em>.</p>
        <p>The patron kings were the primordial Pandya kings. Pandya legitimacy is thus inscribed into the very origin of Tamil literary culture. This is not incidental — it is a political claim embedded in the founding legend. All three Sangams are in Pandya territory.</p>

        <h3>The Second Sangam — இடைச்சங்கம்</h3>
        <p>The Second Sangam met at <strong>Kapatapuram</strong> — also submerged. Duration: <strong>3,700 years</strong>. Members: <strong>59</strong>. From this Sangam comes <strong>Tolkappiyam</strong> — composed by Tolkappiyar, a student of the Agastya tradition. It is the only surviving text from this entire legendary period. The other works of the Second Sangam are lost.</p>

        <h3>The Third Sangam — கடைச்சங்கம்</h3>
        <p>The Third Sangam met at the historical city of <strong>Madurai</strong>. Duration: <strong>1,850 years</strong>. Members: <strong>449</strong>. This is the Sangam whose literature actually survives: the Eight Anthologies, the Ten Idylls, and the minor works that follow.</p>

        <div class="callout callout--critical">
          <div class="callout-icon">⚑</div>
          <div>
            <div class="callout-head">MuVa's Judgment</div>
            <p>Mu. Varadarajan treats the Three Sangams as legend — Tamil civilization's story about itself, not a verifiable historical record. The numbers (4,400 years, 3,700 years, 1,850 years) are mythological. The submerged cities belong to a tradition of lost paradises common across many cultures. What the legend tells us is this: Tamils understood their literature as ancient beyond measure, institutionally organised under royal patronage, and as something that had survived catastrophe. These are civilisational self-understandings, not biographical facts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>What the Legend Conceals</h3>
        <p>The Three Sangam legend is also a political document. By locating the origin of Tamil literature in Pandya territory, under Pandya kings, it makes Pandya cultural authority supreme over Chera and Chola. The legend was systematised and promoted during periods of Pandya political resurgence — particularly the early medieval period. The Cholas, who dominated Tamil political history from the 9th to 13th centuries, are conspicuously absent from the founding narrative of Tamil literature. That absence was deliberate.</p>
        <a href="/works/tolkappiyam" class="read-more-link">Study Tolkappiyam in full →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="period-sidebar">
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">First Sangam</div>
          <p>Tenmaturai (submerged) · 4,400 years · 549 poets · Presided by Agastya · Key work: Agattiyam (lost)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">Second Sangam</div>
          <p>Kapatapuram (submerged) · 3,700 years · 59 poets · Key work: Tolkappiyam (survives)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">Third Sangam</div>
          <p>Madurai (historical) · 1,850 years · 449 poets · Key works: Ettuthokai, Pathupattu</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule" style="margin:0"/>

<!-- PERIOD 02 -->
<section class="hist-period hist-period--alt" id="tolkappiyam">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="period-header">
      <span class="period-num">02</span>
      <div>
        <div class="period-tag">~300–200 BCE (debated)</div>
        <h2 class="period-title">Tolkappiyam <span class="period-ta">தொல்காப்பியம்</span></h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="period-body">
      <div class="period-main prose-wide">
        <p class="lead">The oldest surviving Tamil text. It is a grammar — but to call it only a grammar is like calling the Upanishads only theology. It is simultaneously a phonological system, a morphological analysis, and a complete theory of poetics, aesthetics, and the relationship between human emotion and landscape.</p>

        <h3>The Three Books</h3>
        <p>The first book, <strong>Ezhutthathikaram</strong>, describes Tamil phonology with precision matching modern linguistics in its classification of consonants. The second, <strong>Solladhikaram</strong>, covers morphology and introduces the Tamil grammatical binary: <em>uyartiṇai</em> (the higher class — humans and gods) versus <em>aḵṟiṇai</em> (everything else). Personhood is a grammatical category — there is no equivalent of this binary in Panini or in Greek grammar.</p>
        <p>The third book, <strong>Poruladhikaram</strong>, is where Tolkappiyam becomes extraordinary. It establishes the <strong>tinai system</strong> — five landscape-based emotional categories that organise all Sangam poetry. A poem set in the mountain tinai (Kurinji) is about union; one set in the seashore tinai (Neytal) is about longing and anxiety. The landscape is not a backdrop — it is the emotional argument of the poem.</p>

        <h3>The Political Claim of Tolkappiyam</h3>
        <p>Tolkappiyam is a declaration of Tamil cultural independence from Sanskrit. In the period of its composition, Brahminic Sanskrit was expanding southward — with its Vedas, its Paninian grammar, its ritual literature establishing itself as the prestige system of the subcontinent. Tolkappiyam says: Tamil has its own phonological system, its own morphology, its own aesthetic theory. The claim was foundational — every subsequent debate about Tamil's relationship to Sanskrit goes back to this text.</p>

        <div class="callout callout--note">
          <div class="callout-icon">◈</div>
          <div>
            <div class="callout-head">The Dating Controversy</div>
            <p>When was Tolkappiyam composed? Scholars disagree by several centuries. Some date it to the 5th century BCE; others to the 1st century CE. MuVa accepts a date around 300–200 BCE for the core of the text, while acknowledging that later interpolations entered it. The text has internal linguistic layers — some sections use older Tamil, others use forms suggesting later composition. This reveals it as a living document, refined over time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <a href="/works/tolkappiyam" class="read-more-link">Full study of Tolkappiyam including the complete tinai system →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="period-sidebar">
        <div class="sidebar-box sidebar-box--highlight">
          <div class="sb-label">Key Fact</div>
          <p>Tolkappiyam is the oldest surviving grammar of any Indian language. Panini's Ashtadhyayi is contemporary — but Tolkappiyam's Poruladhikaram has no equivalent in Panini: there is no Sanskrit grammar that also functions as a treatise on the psychology of love and the aesthetics of poetry.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule" style="margin:0"/>

<!-- PERIOD 03 -->
<section class="hist-period" id="sangam-age">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="period-header">
      <span class="period-num">03</span>
      <div>
        <div class="period-tag">300 BCE – 300 CE</div>
        <h2 class="period-title">The Sangam Age <span class="period-ta">சங்க காலம்</span></h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="period-body">
      <div class="period-main prose-wide">
        <p class="lead">The Sangam age is the most extensively documented period in ancient Tamil history — documented not by inscriptions or chronicles but by poetry. The Eight Anthologies and Ten Idylls are simultaneously a literary canon and a historical archive. They tell us what Sangam people ate, how their cities were organised, who their gods were, how they thought about death, how women spoke about desire, and what they valued in a king.</p>

        <h3>The Political World: Three Kingdoms, Constant War</h3>
        <p>The Sangam world was politically organised around three crowned kingdoms: <strong>Chera</strong> (Kerala coast, capital Vanji), <strong>Chola</strong> (Kaveri delta, capital Uraiyur and the port of Puhar/Kaveripattinam), and <strong>Pandya</strong> (far south, capital Madurai). These were not peaceful neighbours. The Purananuru records constant warfare — cities sacked, kings killed in battle, whole territories changing hands.</p>
        <p>Below the three crowned kings were the <strong>Velir chieftains</strong> — minor lords who controlled hill regions and river valleys. The most celebrated was <strong>Pari</strong>, chieftain of the Parambu hills, famous for unconditional generosity. When the three crowned kings conspired to kill Pari because his mountain passes gave him strategic power, the poet Kapilar composed ten elegies of grief and accusation that are still read as the deepest statement of what friendship and loyalty mean. The political murder of a minor chieftain, recorded by a Brahmin court poet, kept alive 2,000 years later — this is what Sangam literature does.</p>

        <h3>Roman Trade and the Material World</h3>
        <p>The first century CE was a period of active trade between Tamil ports and the Roman Empire. <strong>Pliny the Elder</strong> wrote in 77 CE that Rome was losing 50 million sesterces per year to India — mostly through Tamil ports. Roman coins (Augustan, Tiberian, Neronian) have been excavated at Karur, Coimbatore, Madurai, and Arikamedu. The Sangam poem <strong>Pattinapalai</strong> describes the port of Puhar with extraordinary precision: its warehouses, its Yavana (Greek/Roman) merchants carrying torches in the evening streets, its goods of pepper, pearls, and precious stones. Mortimer Wheeler's excavations at Arikamedu in the 1940s found Roman Arretine ware pottery alongside Tamil Black-and-Red ware. The poem and the archaeology confirm each other.</p>

        <h3>The People Who Made the Literature</h3>
        <p><strong>Paanars (பாணர்)</strong> — hereditary bards who traveled between courts, composed and sang for livelihood. The aatrupadai poems are literally one paanan telling another where to find a generous patron. This is the economics of culture, preserved in literature.</p>
        <p><strong>Viraliyar (விரலியர்)</strong> — women performers who traveled with paanars. Their social position was neither slave nor noble — a category later Tamil society would struggle to classify and eventually stigmatise.</p>
        <p><strong>Brahmin scholars</strong> — Kapilar, Nakkirar. But their Brahminism in the Sangam world meant scholarship, not temple ritual. They engaged with politics, grief, and love like everyone else.</p>
        <p><strong>Women of the nobility</strong> — over 30 named women poets in the anthologies. They speak about desire, longing, anger at infidelity, the physical experience of waiting — without shame, without apology. This was the norm in the Sangam world. Sappho of Lesbos is virtually the only named woman poet whose work survives from classical Greece. Tamil literature from the same period has thirty women by name.</p>

        <div class="callout callout--critical">
          <div class="callout-icon">⚑</div>
          <div>
            <div class="callout-head">What Sangam Literature Reveals About Social Order</div>
            <p>The Sangam world was not an egalitarian paradise. The caste system existed. The three-tiered social order of kings, warriors, and cultivators is visible in the poems. Women's freedom was real within a specific class — the poetry of the noble household — and restricted elsewhere. The Viraliyar women had freedoms that palace women did not, but lacked the social protections palace women had. The Sangam world was a specific, complex social order — not a utopia, not a tyranny. Both things need to be said.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>The Eight Anthologies (எட்டுத்தொகை)</h3>
        <div class="anthologies-table">
          {[
            ['Natrinai நற்றிணை','400 poems','Akam — all five tinais','Most "correct" anthology; the classical standard'],
            ['Kurunthokai குறுந்தொகை','401 poems','Akam — love in short form','Fewest words, deepest feeling'],
            ['Ainkurunuru ஐங்குறுநூறு','500 poems','100 per tinai','Musical, patterned — like a song cycle'],
            ['Padirruppattu பதிற்றுப்பத்து','80 poems','Puram — Chera kings only','Two of eight sections lost'],
            ['Purananuru புறநானூறு','400 poems','Puram — war, death, philosophy','Most quoted in modern Tamil'],
            ['Akananuru அகநானூறு','400 poems','Akam — long detailed love poems','Most cinematic; elaborate nature descriptions'],
            ['Kalittokai கலித்தொகை','150 poems','Akam — dramatic dialogues','Lovers argue and tease; the most theatrical'],
            ['Paripatal பரிபாடல்','70 poems (48 lost)','Gods and Madurai','Bridge to Bhakti age; devotional in character'],
          ].map(([name, poems, theme, note]) => (
            <div class="anth-row">
              <div class="anth-name">{name}</div>
              <div class="anth-meta">
                <span class="anth-count">{poems}</span>
                <span class="anth-theme">{theme}</span>
                <span class="anth-note">{note}</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <a href="/sangam" class="read-more-link">Full study of the Sangam period →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="period-sidebar">
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">Key Archaeological Sites</div>
          <p>Arikamedu (Roman trade post) · Adichanallur (Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions) · Kodumanal (trade centre) · Poompuhar (submerged Kaveripattinam)</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">Contemporary World</div>
          <p>While Sangam poetry was being composed: Augustus Caesar rules Rome · The Silk Road is active · Virgil writes the Aeneid · Ovid writes Metamorphoses</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sidebar-box sidebar-box--highlight">
          <div class="sb-label">Women Poets</div>
          <p>30+ named women poets in Sangam anthologies. Sappho is the only named woman poet in all of classical Greece — and most of her work is lost.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule" style="margin:0"/>

<!-- PERIOD 04: KALABHRA -->
<section class="hist-period hist-period--critical" id="kalabhra">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="period-header">
      <span class="period-num">04</span>
      <div>
        <div class="period-tag">~300 CE – 600 CE</div>
        <h2 class="period-title">The Kalabhra Interregnum <span class="period-ta">கலப்பிரர் இடைவெளி</span></h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="period-body">
      <div class="period-main prose-wide">
        <p class="lead">Between the end of the Sangam age and the beginning of the Bhakti movement lies approximately 300 years that later Tamil tradition calls a period of disorder and darkness. They are wrong — or rather, they are politically motivated. The Kalabhra period was not dark. It was different. And that difference threatened the power structures that came after it.</p>

        <h3>Who Were the Kalabhras?</h3>
        <p>The Kalabhras are one of the most debated peoples in South Indian history. Their origin is disputed — some scholars place them as Deccan migrants, others as local chieftains exploiting a power vacuum. What is clear: they displaced all three crowned Tamil kingdoms and ruled Tamil Nadu for approximately 250–300 years, patronising Jainism and Buddhism.</p>
        <p>Later Brahminic and Shaiva sources describe them as illegitimate, as destroyers of Varnashrama (caste order), as the cause of "Kali Yuga" conditions. These descriptions are ideological, not historical. They come from the people who came after the Kalabhras and needed to justify displacing them.</p>

        <h3>What the Kalabhra Period Actually Produced</h3>
        <p>The Five Great Tamil Epics were composed in this environment. <strong>Silappadikaram</strong> and <strong>Manimekalai</strong> (Buddhist-influenced). <strong>Civaka Cintamani</strong> (Jain). The <strong>Thirukkural</strong> — with its Jain-inflected ethics of non-violence, compassion, and non-killing. The <strong>Naladiyar</strong> — 400 quatrains by Jain monks. This was not a literary dark age. It was a period of Jain and Buddhist intellectual dominance producing texts that would become permanent parts of the Tamil canon.</p>

        <h3>The Erasure</h3>
        <p>When the Pallavas drove the Kalabhras out in the 6th century, the subsequent Bhakti movement undertook what we might call a cultural reconquest. Temples given to Jain communities were reclaimed for Shaiva worship. Thirugnanasambandar's hymns explicitly celebrate the defeat of Jains — they describe Jain monks being impaled on stakes. These are not metaphors. The Periyapuranam records actual religious violence against Jain communities as acts of divine piety.</p>
        <p>The Kalabhras themselves left almost no literature that survived — because the people who came after controlled the textual tradition and had no interest in preserving Kalabhra memory. We know of them primarily through their absence and through the literature their opponents wrote about them.</p>

        <div class="callout callout--critical">
          <div class="callout-icon">⚑</div>
          <div>
            <div class="callout-head">The Political Significance of This Erasure</div>
            <p>The Kalabhra erasure is one of the earliest and most complete examples of victors' history in South India. The Bhakti movement did not just defeat the Kalabhras politically — they rewrote the cultural memory of Tamil civilisation to exclude 300 years of Jain and Buddhist intellectual dominance. The Thirukkural survived because it was too important and too ambiguous to destroy — but Jain and Buddhist interpretations of it were marginalised for centuries. This history is consistently underrepresented in TN school textbooks. It happened. It matters.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <a href="/works/thirukkural" class="read-more-link">Study the Thirukkural — product of this period →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="period-sidebar">
        <div class="sidebar-box sidebar-box--critical">
          <div class="sb-label">Historical Honesty</div>
          <p>The Kalabhra period is consistently underrepresented in Tamil literary education because it disrupts the narrative of continuous Hindu-Tamil civilisation. This site presents it as it was.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">Works from this period</div>
          <p>Thirukkural · Silappadikaram · Manimekalai · Civaka Cintamani · Naladiyar · Pathinenkilkanakku</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule" style="margin:0"/>

<!-- PERIOD 05: BHAKTI -->
<section class="hist-period" id="bhakti-age">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="period-header">
      <span class="period-num">05</span>
      <div>
        <div class="period-tag">600 CE – 900 CE</div>
        <h2 class="period-title">The Bhakti Age <span class="period-ta">பக்தி காலம்</span></h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="period-body">
      <div class="period-main prose-wide">
        <p class="lead">The Bhakti movement produced some of the most emotionally intense poetry in any language in any century. It also served a political function that its participants may or may not have been fully conscious of. Both facts are equally true. Separating them is the work of honest scholarship.</p>

        <h3>The Political Function of the Bhakti Saints</h3>
        <p>When the Pallavas displaced the Kalabhras, they needed to establish cultural legitimacy alongside military dominance. The Nayanmars provided it. They traveled to every Shaiva temple in Tamil Nadu, composed hymns, gathered followers, and thereby established those temples — and the kings who protected them — as centres of sacred authority. <strong>Thirugnanasambandar's</strong> conversion of the Pandya king from Jainism to Shaivism was a political event — a change of royal religious patronage — wrapped in a hagiographic narrative. <strong>Appar</strong> was imprisoned by the Pallava king Mahendravarman I, a Jain convert, and eventually secured his conversion back to Shaivism. The literary record of that imprisonment — Appar's prison hymns — is simultaneously great poetry and a political document.</p>

        <h3>The 63 Nayanmars — Diversity and Its Limits</h3>
        <p>The 63 Nayanmars came from across the social spectrum. <strong>Nandanar</strong> was from a low-caste community that swept temple floors. <strong>Kannappar</strong> was a hunter who fed the Shiva linga with meat and offered his own eyes when the linga bled. These stories of cross-caste devotion were genuine and revolutionary within Bhakti theology. But the institutionalisation of the movement ultimately reproduced and sometimes reinforced caste hierarchy within temple administration. The revolutionary impulse was real. Its institutional transformation was also real.</p>

        <h3>Andal — Woman, Saint, Scripture</h3>
        <p><strong>Andal</strong> is the only woman among the 12 Alvars. She wore temple garlands before they were offered to the deity (ritually polluting them by Brahminic standards, sanctifying them by devotional logic), refused human marriage, and composed the <strong>Thirupavai</strong> — 30 verses still recited at 4:30 AM every morning in December in Vaishnava temples across the Tamil world. That a woman's composition is the daily liturgical opening of temple worship 1,200 years after her death is a fact worth sitting with.</p>

        <div class="callout callout--note">
          <div class="callout-icon">◈</div>
          <div>
            <div class="callout-head">Contemporary World</div>
            <p>700 CE: Muhammad's death; Islam begins its expansion. Europe in the Carolingian period — almost no original vernacular literature. Tang Dynasty China at its cultural peak. Tamil Bhakti poetry is among the world's great literary achievements of this century. That it is not in the Western canon says everything about the canon, not about the poetry.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <a href="/poets/manikkavacakar" class="read-more-link">Read Manikkavacakar — the Bhakti age's most intense voice →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="period-sidebar">
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">The Three Canonical Nayanmars</div>
          <p>Thirugnanasambandar · Appar (Thirunavukkarasar) · Sundarar — their hymns form the Tevaram, the core of Shaiva Tamil scripture</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sidebar-box">
          <div class="sb-label">The 12 Alvars include</div>
          <p>Nammalvar · Andal (only woman) · Periyalvar · Thirumangai Alvar · and 8 others — composers of the 4,000-verse Nalayira Divya Prabandham</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule" style="margin:0"/>

<!-- PERIOD 06: MEDIEVAL -->
<section class="hist-period hist-period--alt" id="medieval-age">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="period-header">
      <span class="period-num">06</span>
      <div>
        <div class="period-tag">900 CE – 1300 CE</div>
        <h2 class="period-title">The Medieval Period <span class="period-ta">இடைக்கால இலக்கியம்</span></h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="period-body">
      <div class="period-main prose-wide">
        <p class="lead">The medieval period coincides with the height of the Chola empire — the greatest Tamil political power ever assembled. Under Rajaraja Chola I (985–1014 CE) and Rajendra Chola I (1012–1044 CE), Tamil armies reached Sri Lanka, the Maldives, and maritime Southeast Asia. Literature in this period had a new job: not to record a world, but to construct one.</p>

        <h3>Kambaramayanam — The Tamil Ramayana</h3>
        <p><strong>Kambar's Kambaramayanam</strong> (12th century CE) is not a translation of Valmiki's Ramayana. It is a reimagining — over 10,000 verses in the viruttam metre, with a Rama defined by love rather than duty, and a Ravana who is genuinely great rather than simply evil. Kambar allows Ravana dignity in defeat. In the Chola period, when the Ramayana was being used as an imperial ideological text (Rama as the ideal king), Kambar's sympathetic Ravana complicates the allegory. Power that crosses a moral line loses legitimacy — but that does not erase the greatness that preceded the crossing.</p>

        <h3>Periyapuranam — The Lives of the Saints</h3>
        <p><strong>Sekkizhar's Periyapuranam</strong> (12th century CE) — the life stories of all 63 Nayanmars — was commissioned by the Chola king Kulottunga II. 4,281 verses. It is simultaneously a literary masterpiece, a religious text, and a piece of Chola imperial propaganda. The stories of cross-caste devotion (Nandanar, Kannappar) were genuinely radical in their implications. The Chola use of those stories was more conservative: they demonstrated the king's Shaiva legitimacy by sponsoring the literature that glorified Shaiva saints. The radical implication and the conservative use coexist in the same text. This is how power works with art.</p>

        <div class="callout callout--note">
          <div class="callout-icon">◈</div>
          <div>
            <div class="callout-head">End of the Classical Period</div>
            <p>The Chola empire declined after the 13th century. The subsequent period of Tamil literature (from ~1300 CE) is outside the classical canon defined by MuVa. It includes Shaiva Siddhanta theological literature, Vijayanagara-period works, and colonial-period modernisation — important, but belonging to a different study.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <a href="/poets/kambar" class="read-more-link">Read the study of Kambar →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="period-sidebar">
        <div class="sidebar-box sidebar-box--highlight">
          <div class="sb-label">Chola Empire at Its Height</div>
          <p>Rajaraja I builds Thanjavur's Brihadeesvarar temple (completed 1010 CE) — largest in India at the time. Rajendra I's navy raids Srivijaya (Sumatra) in 1025 CE. Tamil cultural and military reach extends from Sri Lanka to Southeast Asia.</p>
